  "live_records": {
    "q-70133": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70133",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$",
      "worked_solution": "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+3\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
